[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Motorola are going to be supported by Graphene OS, which is awesome. &gt;&gt; For nerds. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; Proton employees are nerds, but they also work on privacy tools every day. But what digital [music] tools do they'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually use? Let\'s ask Harley. &gt;&gt; Hi, I\'m Harley. I\'m a privacy expert at Proton and today I\'m going to show you some of the digital privacy and security tools I use in my actual life. Graphene OS'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Graphene OS is a modified version of Android, but [music] it strips out all of Google\'s tracking and then hardens the security way further than any [music] normal operating system.'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Graphene by default doesn\'t come with any Google apps pre-installed. [music] One of the features is you get complete control over an app\'s permission. [music] You don\'t want it to have access to the'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'internet, you can block that. These are all the permissions you can control from any of the apps, anywhere from location data to your calendar. Take this earbud app for example, it doesn\'t really need'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'access to the internet. You\'re just controlling your music. [music] If you go into app info and then go down to permissions, click on networking, click don\'t allow, blocks network [music] access, so it can\'t send your data to'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some random server or whatever. Another feature &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; is something called Durress Pin. You have your normal PIN to unlock your phone, but if you enter the Durress Pin,'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; it will erase your phone completely, leaving no data on there. This feature is so powerful, the US government uh currently trying to imprison someone for using it.'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Qubes OS Imagine you could separate your life onto different machines. You have your personal one, you have your work one, and then you have one for your private'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data, like half and whatnot. That\'s exactly what Qubes OS does. But instead of being multiple machines, it does it all in one. Qubes OS takes the concept of what if you could separate'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your life into different machines, and makes it way more convenient by using virtual machines. One device, many OSs. Even though it appears everything is running on the same machine, they\'re all'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'isolated into different virtual machines. All my work stuff is highlighted with a blue border. All my personal stuff is this yellow border. And then my vault is this gray border. It\'s a visual indication of where you\'re'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working. If I accidentally open something malicious on my personal environment, it can\'t affect my work environment. Basically, it\'s the digital equivalent of not putting all your eggs'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in one basket. &gt;&gt; Tor browser. [music] &gt;&gt; When people hear Tor, they often think dark web. And sure, that does exist. But it is used by so many other people'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to protect their privacy, their families\' privacy. For some, it can literally [music] be a matter of life and death. As you can see, Tor is just a normal web'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browser. But it does a lot of things differently. Unlike a VPN, where the connection just gets sent through one server, Tor has this three-hop approach. The first'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'server knows where the traffic came from. The second one doesn\'t know where it\'s come from or where it\'s going. And then the third server only knows the end destination. By the time your request'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reaches the last server, no one knows where that came from. [music] To be clear, it isn\'t the same three servers. There are thousands of servers it could be sent through. Proton actually hosts'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their own hidden services. You can go to proton.me on the Tor browser and you should see a little button that says onion available. Click on that and it\'ll take you to the Tor hidden service. One of the other important parts of the Tor'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browser is it tries to make every web session look the same. So, you can\'t be distinguished from any other Tor user. One of the tools they use, for example, is letterboxing. So, you see how this window doesn\'t go right'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the edges? It\'s to make everything look identical so you\'re not identifiable. To be clear, Tor can be slow sometimes, but like with all privacy tools, there\'s a bit of give'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and take. You give up some convenience or speed or both for some more privacy. And in my eyes, that\'s a deal I\'m willing to take. &gt;&gt; Organic Maps'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; I don\'t use Google Maps, nor do I use Apple Maps. So, how the hell did I get myself to the studio today? I use Organic Maps. It\'s a free and open-source'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; mapping software that uses OpenStreetMap data. You download the maps directly [music] to your phone and it keeps all the data local. No ads, no tracking. My phone is actually on airplane mode. I\'m just'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'using GPS. Let\'s say I want to find, I don\'t know, the nearest pub. I can search pub and scroll down. Ah, there is one right across the road.'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; It\'s private match, &gt;&gt; RSS Reader &gt;&gt; This is probably one of the least cybersecurity tools on this list, but it\'s the one that keeps big tech from'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rotting my brain. Most content that you find is pushed upon you by the algorithm. An RSS reader takes a different approach. You subscribe to the feed and it shows you what they\'ve'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'released [music] in chronological order. Instead of going to five different apps, it\'s [music] all in my RSS reader. Basically, I am the algorithm and that\'s exactly how much control I should have.'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] Whether you\'re going from YouTube to some news outlet or some magazine, it puts it all into one space. &gt;&gt; F-Droid'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; It\'s so cute. We have this idea that we\'re stuck with the app store that comes on our phone. But on Android, you do have another option and it\'s called F-Droid. [music] It\'s a app store that hosts exclusively'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open-source applications. [music] These are applications that respect your privacy. I know us privacy nerds talk about open-source every 5 seconds, but there is a reason. When the code\'s open,'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they can verify the claims developers make on whether they track you and what information they collect because that code is visible. F-Droid itself is also open-source. If a developer\'s no longer'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'able to continue working on that app, other people from the community can pick up where they left off along with contributing &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; new features and fixes to the app. On'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'F-Droid, you can find a whole bunch of apps, anything from board games to browsers. You can also find some Proton\'s apps on F-Droid because everything we do is'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open-source. It\'s a nice reminder that even something as basic as where you get your apps [music] doesn\'t need to be the default choice. All this being said, Google thinks this is too powerful. They\'re'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trying to shut this down. And that\'s called a monopoly. You can learn more about this at keepandroidopen.org. &gt;&gt; Keep mobile. &gt;&gt; We talked about your phone\'s operating'

[07:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system, maps, [music] apps, but there one more piece of the puzzle, and that\'s your cellular provider. Ke Mobile is different. It was built from the ground up to respect your privacy. Instead of trying to collect everything about you,'

[07:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like your address, your social security number, even your friends aunt\'s cat\'s name, they collect very minimal data. They take your payment info and your zip code, and that\'s about it. One of the'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cool things with Kate is you can actually get additional phone numbers for free. You can have your normal number that you give out to friends and family, but you can also have one for'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'random spam. In the last video we recorded, I talked about something [music] called a stingray or an IMSI-catcher. Ke Mobile actually has this cool feature where [music] you can rotate your IMSI'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'number. So, I actually have mine set up to rotate every day [music] at a certain time. If this whole IMSI thing makes no sense to you, you should check out our previous video where we go into a little bit more detail. Quick disclaimer,'

